WEBVTT

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:45.370
3, 2, 1, 0, launch, commit, we have a liftoff, all engines building up thrust, moving out, clear the tower.

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:48.380
Tower roger, tower clear.

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:51.660
Roger Tom, you got good thrust on all engines, you're right on the money.

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:54.720
30 seconds, we're on the way.

00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:57.880
Stand by for mode 1 bravo, mark 1 bravo.

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:00.000
1 bravo, 2 G's.

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:03.080
Roger.

00:07:03.730 --> 00:07:05.300
Apollo, Houston, you're a go for staging.

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:06.740
Roger, go for staging.

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:29.170
Control centers, Moscow and Houston, have given a go for docking.

00:07:29.170 --> 00:07:30.000
Hello, Houston, Apollo.

00:07:30.090 --> 00:07:33.760
825 by 5.

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:36.760
Apollo, Houston, I got two messages for you.

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:38.710
Moscow is go for docking.

00:07:38.710 --> 00:07:39.710
Houston is go for docking.

00:07:39.710 --> 00:07:40.710
It's up to you guys.

00:07:40.710 --> 00:07:41.620
Have fun.

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:44.260
All righty, sounds good.

00:07:44.260 --> 00:07:46.620
Apollo, meet our electric.

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:48.620
Soyuz docking system is ready.

00:07:48.620 --> 00:07:49.890
Good job.

00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:51.890
Let's stay there.

00:07:51.890 --> 00:07:55.020
I am approaching Soyuz.

00:07:55.020 --> 00:08:00.000
Tom, please don't forget about your engine.

00:08:01.340 --> 00:08:03.040
Less than five meters distance.

00:08:04.980 --> 00:08:05.770
Three meters.

00:08:06.170 --> 00:08:06.790
Three meters.

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:12.030
One meter.

00:08:12.210 --> 00:08:12.630
One meter.

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:17.940
I'm here.

00:08:18.310 --> 00:08:18.710
Contact.

00:08:18.710 --> 00:08:19.810
You have capture.

00:08:20.710 --> 00:08:21.470
Capture.

00:08:23.290 --> 00:08:26.580
Tell Professor Bushuyev it was a soft docking.

00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:29.710
Well done, Tom. It was a good show.

00:08:30.510 --> 00:08:36.850
They are looking forward now to second hand vision in both of you.

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:39.400
All right, on a show.

00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:40.740
I'll provide you loop three.

00:08:41.310 --> 00:08:42.090
Okay, the camera.

00:08:42.270 --> 00:08:42.710
Ha-ha!

00:08:43.610 --> 00:08:45.190
That's just it there.

00:08:46.490 --> 00:08:47.020
Got it?

00:08:48.520 --> 00:08:49.840
It'll stay open.

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:52.800
Go ahead.

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:53.660
Go ahead, Tom.

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:57.450
Lexi, just a moment.

00:08:59.110 --> 00:09:00.000
Okay, turn on.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:27.810
new possibilities are opening up for fruitful development of scientific cooperation between

00:09:27.810 --> 00:09:30.000
countries and the peoples in the interest of

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:34.150
peace and progress of all humanity.

00:09:34.150 --> 00:09:38.990
I wish you successful completion of the planned program

00:09:40.290 --> 00:09:44.180
and a safe return to Earth.

00:09:44.180 --> 00:09:47.830
Leonid Brezhnev.

00:09:47.830 --> 00:09:50.200
- Astronauts are on the line, sir.

00:09:50.200 --> 00:09:55.280
Gentlemen, let me call to express my very great admiration

00:09:55.910 --> 00:10:00.000
for your hard work, your total dedication in preparing

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:07.910
this first joint flight. It's taken us many years to open this door to useful

00:10:07.910 --> 00:10:13.930
cooperation in space between our two countries and I'm confident that the day

00:10:13.930 --> 00:10:23.500
is not far off when space missions made possible by this first joint effort will

00:10:23.500 --> 00:10:30.000
be more or less commonplace. And may I say in signing off, here's to a

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:31.000
a soft landing.

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:40.840
The total system we have demonstrated here, the new docking device, the rendezvous system

00:10:40.840 --> 00:10:46.590
using techniques in both countries, the communications and procedures and techniques could be available

00:10:46.590 --> 00:10:51.390
in the future if required, not on an instance notice, but it could be available.

00:10:51.390 --> 00:10:56.750
So I think we have taken a great step, that indeed we have opened a new era in the history

00:10:56.750 --> 00:10:59.300
of man on this, and it will be beneficial.

00:12:04.960 --> 00:12:12.020
Fino. Go. Guido. Go. Retro. Go, flight. GNC. Go, flight.

00:12:12.020 --> 00:12:18.220
Apollo Control, 99 hours, 29 minutes, phase elapsed time. Flight Director Frank Littleton

00:12:18.220 --> 00:12:23.770
pulsing his flight controllers. E-com reports everything looks fine. Go for the bird.

00:12:23.770 --> 00:12:27.550
Okay, Crip, everything's up. The great ship up here. The only thing we're concerned about

00:12:27.550 --> 00:12:30.000
is that you've got all your splashdown parties coordinated.

00:12:30.760 --> 00:12:33.800
- Well, I've been working on that.

00:12:33.800 --> 00:12:35.640
- Okay, sounds good.

00:13:26.910 --> 00:13:30.000
You and Valery Kubasov were selected for a project that

00:13:30.090 --> 00:13:35.970
many would say was as significant as perhaps landing on the moon that to link

00:13:35.970 --> 00:13:41.600
up your Soyuz vehicle with the American Apollo spacecraft in this technical

00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:47.560
concept and a born out of a political gesture and a technical demonstration.

00:13:47.560 --> 00:13:55.090
What did you think of this project, what its goals were, and what you thought the

00:13:55.090 --> 00:14:00.000
accomplishments would be when you and Valeri were selected to represent

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:15.230
I can only say that I'm very glad that the government, while addressing different countries,

00:14:15.230 --> 00:14:21.810
our government said that the crew performed flawlessly, flawless.

00:14:21.810 --> 00:14:26.030
That's the actual word they used.

00:14:26.030 --> 00:14:30.000
And we should not forget about the specifics.

00:14:30.060 --> 00:14:34.590
of that particular time, Cold War.

00:14:34.590 --> 00:14:39.750
The humankind was at the precipice, literally.

00:14:39.750 --> 00:14:46.760
But we were lucky to have had very smart people,

00:14:46.760 --> 00:14:53.590
Kissinger, Kosygin, Fletcher, who understood the situation

00:14:53.590 --> 00:14:56.010
that was going on on the ground.

00:14:56.010 --> 00:15:00.000
And they managed to make certain steps

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:08.500
to have fixed this situation, to grow some seed of trust that people manage to treat

00:15:08.500 --> 00:15:10.220
each other differently.

00:15:10.220 --> 00:15:17.220
I think this program turned out to be a lot more important for our countries, and particularly

00:15:17.220 --> 00:15:25.330
the political aspects, because six billion people were watching us, and they could realize

00:15:25.330 --> 00:15:28.230
that there is a way to live differently.

00:15:28.230 --> 00:15:30.000
There is a way to deal

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:32.480
with each other in a different manner.

00:15:32.480 --> 00:15:38.900
Well, actually, you and your crewmates felt at the time that you were about to do something

00:15:38.900 --> 00:15:45.400
very significant, I think, that would change U.S.-Russian relations, didn't you?

00:15:45.400 --> 00:15:49.000
That's exactly what it was.

00:15:49.000 --> 00:16:00.000
I strongly felt that I received a task which was really a task with huge magnitude.

00:16:00.060 --> 00:16:06.580
I was supposed to show the entire world that we are human beings, that we work together

00:16:06.580 --> 00:16:11.840
in the most sophisticated area of human activities.

00:16:11.840 --> 00:16:17.770
It was a very important task, and the rest of the people involved in the preparations

00:16:17.770 --> 00:16:22.810
for this flight, they were thinking exactly along the same lines.

00:16:22.810 --> 00:16:28.470
And there were thousands, literally thousands of people in both countries who were involved,

00:16:28.470 --> 00:16:30.000
and they made everything they could.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:33.760
and made it really conducive for us to succeed.

00:16:34.640 --> 00:16:38.880
And prior to the launch and during the docking,

00:16:39.040 --> 00:16:42.240
everything was designed on the ground

00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:47.640
between our countries and our respective experts

00:16:47.640 --> 00:16:52.080
from both countries, which let us actually succeed.

00:16:52.980 --> 00:16:57.180
Alexei, many, many people say that the close personal bond

00:16:57.180 --> 00:16:59.600
that you forged with Tom Stafford

00:17:00.220 --> 00:17:04.780
was the key to the success of Apollo Soyuz.

00:17:04.780 --> 00:17:06.780
You call each other brothers to this day.

00:17:06.780 --> 00:17:08.920
You correspond with one another frequently.

00:17:08.920 --> 00:17:11.160
You talk to one another frequently.

00:17:11.160 --> 00:17:13.700
What was the most important factor

00:17:13.700 --> 00:17:16.730
that emerged from your personality,

00:17:16.730 --> 00:17:18.570
Tom Stafford's personality,

00:17:18.570 --> 00:17:21.820
that created a friendship and a bond

00:17:21.820 --> 00:17:23.860
that made this mission successful

00:17:23.860 --> 00:17:28.860
and made you lifelong brothers?

00:17:28.860 --> 00:17:30.000
- Way prior to

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:40.740
The Soyuz Apollo, just pretty much like media always do, they select the best players during

00:17:40.740 --> 00:17:49.980
the hockey tournament or the best crew members that deserve flying.

00:17:49.980 --> 00:17:59.960
And when we have started doing this similar effort, media folks ended up assigning the

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:09.400
Stafford and myself as a best crew, and I met him for the first time in 1970.

00:18:09.400 --> 00:18:18.210
He came to Russia to be in Moscow during the funeral of my backup crew, Dobrovolsky, Volkov

00:18:18.210 --> 00:18:19.210
and Podsaev.

00:18:19.210 --> 00:18:27.640
Here he decided to come to Moscow by himself, although the State Department did not allow

00:18:27.640 --> 00:18:30.000
him to do so.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:38.640
kinds of things used to be frowned upon back then, but he came to Moscow anyway, and I was

00:18:38.640 --> 00:18:46.180
asked to join him and to spend more time with him while in Moscow, and I started taking

00:18:46.180 --> 00:18:47.120
care of him.

00:18:47.120 --> 00:18:55.720
He's been calling me a brother for a long, long time, and I don't have any close relatives,

00:18:55.720 --> 00:18:56.720
no parents.

00:18:56.720 --> 00:19:00.000
They all are gone.

00:19:00.090 --> 00:19:11.160
when you have all of a sudden a brother it's it just makes the world for me and

00:19:11.160 --> 00:19:17.620
we have common children I helped Tom adopt two sons from Russia and now they

00:19:17.620 --> 00:19:24.740
are grown-up guys their students their surname is Stafford my

00:19:24.740 --> 00:19:30.000
granddaughter his name is Karina just the same way as

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:33.420
Thomas' granddaughter.

00:19:33.420 --> 00:19:35.040
And we have a lot in common.

00:19:35.040 --> 00:19:39.540
We meet two or three times a year.

00:19:39.540 --> 00:19:47.370
Alexei, when you look back at Apollo-Soyuz four decades ago, you look today at the International

00:19:47.370 --> 00:19:53.540
Space Station, which many people believe would not have been possible without Apollo-Soyuz.

00:19:53.540 --> 00:20:00.000
What do you see as the legacy of your career, that particular flight?

00:20:00.890 --> 00:20:06.270
And where are we going from here in the future?

00:20:06.270 --> 00:20:10.970
- After our flight, there was another program,

00:20:10.970 --> 00:20:17.890
a joint program, Shuttle Mir program.

00:20:17.890 --> 00:20:21.690
They have done everything very accurately,

00:20:21.690 --> 00:20:23.880
which was basically the continuation

00:20:23.880 --> 00:20:27.680
of what we have started.

00:20:27.680 --> 00:20:30.000
And it was a part of cooperation

00:20:30.030 --> 00:20:38.680
between USA and Russia, and then we logically arrived at the idea of putting together an

00:20:38.680 --> 00:20:50.600
international space station, 400 tons of weight, one and a half hectare of solar arrays, 1,000

00:20:50.600 --> 00:20:59.820
cubic meters of habitable space, Russian segment, U.S. segment, European segment, all

00:20:59.820 --> 00:21:00.000
WE'LL TAKE A LOOK.

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:06.160
together 7 modules, Russian segment 5 modules, and this is the area where we have permanent

00:21:06.160 --> 00:21:14.620
presence of 3 to 6 crew members at all times, and we used to have as many as 9 crew members

00:21:14.620 --> 00:21:15.620
at some point.

00:21:15.620 --> 00:21:20.080
And this is continuation of what we have been doing all our lives.

00:21:20.080 --> 00:21:25.500
And I was actively working in the area of training of those crew members, eventually

00:21:25.500 --> 00:21:28.320
getting to the International Space Station.

00:21:42.760 --> 00:21:46.080
We're honored today to be joined by General Tom Stafford, a legendary figure

00:21:46.080 --> 00:21:49.640
in human spaceflight history, commander of Apollo-Soyuz,

00:21:49.640 --> 00:21:52.800
whose 40th anniversary is coming up here very shortly.

00:21:52.800 --> 00:21:55.840
What did you think about Apollo-Soyuz when it was first proposed,

00:21:55.840 --> 00:22:00.000
and what did you think the ultimate objective would be?

00:22:00.060 --> 00:22:06.400
thinking so much where, how far, way down the road it would lead, as I was, well, we

00:22:06.400 --> 00:22:13.830
can demonstrate to the world that two countries with different languages, different units

00:22:13.830 --> 00:22:21.640
of measurements, and certainly vastly different political systems could work together for

00:22:21.640 --> 00:22:22.400
a common goal.

00:22:23.020 --> 00:22:24.840
But this would be a good thing.

00:22:24.900 --> 00:22:30.000
It could demonstrate and help a lot of understanding in the world.

00:22:30.440 --> 00:22:32.890
on what we're doing.

00:22:32.890 --> 00:22:38.400
Because of the close personal bond that you and Alexei Leonov forged, many say that that

00:22:38.400 --> 00:22:41.180
was the ultimate success to Apollo-Soyuz.

00:22:41.180 --> 00:22:48.600
What was the most important factor between the two of you that emerged that bonded a friendship

00:22:48.600 --> 00:22:52.600
like no other we've ever seen in human spaceflight history?

00:22:52.600 --> 00:22:58.880
Knowing that this mission had to be a success and all that, just our personalities, it worked

00:22:58.880 --> 00:22:59.960
out great.

00:23:00.120 --> 00:23:02.500
Today I consider Alexi like a brother to me.

00:23:02.500 --> 00:23:09.640
I was an only child, so I call him Waybrot, and I'll tell you how close the friendship

00:23:09.640 --> 00:23:11.600
has gotten.

00:23:11.600 --> 00:23:18.920
His granddaughter was named Kareen, and that's the name after my daughter, and when my oldest

00:23:18.920 --> 00:23:24.810
daughter had her last boy, her last son, she named him Alexi.

00:23:24.810 --> 00:23:26.810
You were the master of rendezvous.

00:23:26.810 --> 00:23:30.000
You had pulled off a number of rendezvous during Gemini and

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:35.420
Apollo, of course, so from that aspect I don't think you believed that that was

00:23:35.420 --> 00:23:40.170
going to be a big issue. What did you think was going to be the most intricate

00:23:40.170 --> 00:23:45.990
part of this mission from a technical standpoint? Well, from a technical

00:23:45.990 --> 00:23:53.660
standpoint, two things: one, they flew at an atmosphere of sea level pressure, you

00:23:53.660 --> 00:24:00.000
know, 21% oxygen, 79% nitrogen, where we flew with five pounds per

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:05.840
square inch total of pure oxygen and then how we had to work this together and

00:24:05.840 --> 00:24:11.950
so yes or the docking module came in good we also use it as an airlock to go

00:24:11.950 --> 00:24:16.100
up and down and we got them to lower their pressure down to you know over a

00:24:16.100 --> 00:24:21.010
period of two days to ten the lower ten pounds and this doesn't hurt you just

00:24:21.010 --> 00:24:27.330
regular atmospheric mixture so that would that was one thing the other one

00:24:27.330 --> 00:24:30.000
was on the docking mechanism and the

00:24:30.440 --> 00:24:34.920
The main thing was not the engaged part, but the latching and the hold part.

00:24:35.500 --> 00:24:36.700
But it worked out beautifully.

00:24:38.170 --> 00:24:39.770
And, of course, on the other side of the coin,

00:24:39.890 --> 00:24:45.350
never far away was the whole importance of the political aspect of this.

00:24:45.350 --> 00:24:49.440
And I just can't even imagine how you and your crewmates

00:24:49.440 --> 00:24:52.970
dealt with the political pressure of making this a success.

00:24:53.780 --> 00:25:00.000
Well, what we said, we thought that this,

00:25:00.510 --> 00:25:05.230
would be, you know, just like a standard mission.

00:25:06.310 --> 00:25:07.770
You think just like a test pilot.

00:25:08.880 --> 00:25:14.080
And so we made it a point, and so did the Soviet side.

00:25:14.310 --> 00:25:16.710
We kept politics completely out of it.

00:25:17.410 --> 00:25:18.990
We never discussed politics.

00:25:19.510 --> 00:25:23.060
It was just the mission, and we were going to make it a success.

00:25:24.780 --> 00:25:26.740
And then there's a moment of history, docking.

00:25:26.740 --> 00:25:29.090
So the two vehicles docked.

00:25:30.030 --> 00:25:32.030
You're joined together, first time in history.

00:25:32.650 --> 00:25:35.430
Did you feel, at that point, a sense of history at all?

00:25:35.790 --> 00:25:36.050
No.

00:25:37.000 --> 00:25:40.340
The main thing was to be sure we got the soft dock.

00:25:41.000 --> 00:25:43.600
And I greased it in, just right on.

00:25:44.610 --> 00:25:49.020
So there, and then we had to have the hard dock with the other, the light, to retract.

00:25:49.520 --> 00:25:51.990
And those lights just pull in and snap.

00:25:53.030 --> 00:25:55.220
And we got to make sure everything was solid.

00:25:55.300 --> 00:25:58.270
And so the hatch is open.

00:25:58.620 --> 00:25:59.540
There's Alexei.

00:26:00.410 --> 00:26:05.710
And you both seemed to be a bit reluctant to move toward one another until you all coaxed

00:26:05.710 --> 00:26:08.610
each other to shake hands.

00:26:08.610 --> 00:26:09.500
What a moment.

00:26:09.500 --> 00:26:15.690
Was that one of the greatest moments of your spaceflight career at that point?

00:26:15.690 --> 00:26:17.090
I think so.

00:26:17.090 --> 00:26:18.490
I think so.

00:26:18.490 --> 00:26:25.940
I've had several great moments, you know, doing the first rendezvous in space, flying

00:26:25.940 --> 00:26:30.000
the lunar module, the first rendezvous around the moon.

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:35.790
Cernan's first spacewalk. Getting him back in was a great moment, I'll tell you. And

00:26:35.790 --> 00:26:42.260
so there's a few ones. And definitely, you're right. That was really one of the great moments.

00:26:42.260 --> 00:26:48.800
But again, no thought of politics at that point. Here an American and a Soviet,

00:26:48.800 --> 00:26:54.170
former adversaries, are shaking hands in space together. Did you not feel some political

00:26:54.170 --> 00:27:00.000
accomplishment as well as just the technical, amazing aspect of reaching this

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:01.800
good friend and brother of yours.

00:27:01.800 --> 00:27:10.280
Well, for some reason politics, because to me, here was a kind of a fellow crewman

00:27:10.280 --> 00:27:17.510
and also his other person, Valeri, who had worked hard with us for two years, and we'd

00:27:17.510 --> 00:27:18.510
accomplished it.

00:27:18.510 --> 00:27:20.200
We'd done it.

00:27:20.200 --> 00:27:22.240
We'd worked out in teamwork together.

00:27:22.240 --> 00:27:29.820
You know, this mission on Apollo-Soyuz 40 years ago is still considered by many.

00:27:30.320 --> 00:27:34.320
almost universally as the key, the birthplace, if you will,

00:27:34.480 --> 00:27:37.860
to the development and construction of the International Space Station.

00:27:38.690 --> 00:27:42.350
Did you ever believe that a laboratory of that magnitude

00:27:42.350 --> 00:27:46.290
and capability, a city in the sky, if you will, could be built

00:27:46.290 --> 00:27:48.730
and operated on a permanently occupied basis?

00:27:49.960 --> 00:27:55.430
No, I knew we'd do things in the future. I was very optimistic we'd go on and do some more

00:27:55.430 --> 00:28:00.000
things, like the shuttle was planned, and we'd have maybe a shuttle

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:05.070
to a Soyuz or maybe a new spacecraft would do something like that.

00:28:05.070 --> 00:28:11.900
To the magnitude that the space station is today, nearly a million pounds, 15 countries

00:28:11.900 --> 00:28:12.900
working together.

00:28:12.900 --> 00:28:19.990
No, I didn't, because, you know, just the United States and Russia, pardon me, the Soviet

00:28:19.990 --> 00:28:23.260
Union was the only one that had the capability.

00:28:23.260 --> 00:28:29.510
So, but I think it's wonderful and I'm very glad, honored I'm still involved.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:39.640
with the program and I chair the advisory committee for the International Space Station.

00:28:39.640 --> 00:28:41.700
That's for safety and operational readiness.

00:28:41.700 --> 00:28:46.970
And it really started out with Shuttle Mirror, which is a natural extension, and then from

00:28:46.970 --> 00:28:51.450
Shuttle Mirror to the International Space Station.

00:28:51.450 --> 00:28:56.930
This fall will mark the 15th anniversary of a permanent human occupancy on the space

00:28:56.930 --> 00:28:59.640
station, which is remarkable in its own right.

00:29:00.540 --> 00:29:07.770
As you look back in the homestretch of the first two decades of the space station, what

00:29:07.770 --> 00:29:13.540
do you think is the most important accomplishment of this complex, this global project?

00:29:13.540 --> 00:29:22.120
Well, to me the most important part is bringing these 15 countries together and having been

00:29:22.120 --> 00:29:30.000
able to agree to work on things and see how, in a way, how flawlessly it's gone. I look

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:35.930
now and the perspective back, say, over my shoulder in history. So I think it was really

00:29:35.930 --> 00:29:41.980
wonderful. I had the opportunity to work with Alexei Leonov, and Valery Kubasov, the other

00:29:41.980 --> 00:29:46.600
Soviet people, it's now Russian, and bring this together.
